<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edward Vernon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1684">1684</a>–<a href="/years/1757">1757</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Old Grog</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edward Vernon was a British admiral in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-jenkins-ear">War of Jenkins' Ear</a>. He captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portobelo">Portobelo</a> with six ships in 1739, a success celebrated across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, but his great assault on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cartagena-colombia">Cartagena de Indias</a> in 1741 failed against the defences led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blas-de-lezo">Blas de Lezo</a> and Viceroy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sebastian-de-eslava">Sebastián de Eslava</a>. He introduced the watered rum ration known as grog.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edward-vernon.jpg" alt="Edward Vernon" width="320" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Gainsborough · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edward_Vernon_by_Thomas_Gainsborough.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Vernon was born in Westminster on 12 November 1684, the second son of James Vernon, secretary of state to William III. He briefly attended Westminster School, an education unusual for naval officers of his day, and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> on 10 May 1700. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a> he served in the flagship of Admiral Cloudesley Shovell at the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a> and the Battle of Málaga, and at the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> in 1705. He was made captain on 22 January 1706 and in 1710 broke up a Spanish squadron off Cartagena.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After service in the Baltic and as commodore on the Jamaica Station in 1720, Vernon became Member of Parliament for Penryn, a seat his father had held, and took a leading part in naval debates. He took up the case of Robert Jenkins, a merchant seaman who claimed his ear had been cut off by Spanish coast guards in 1731. Promoted vice admiral of the blue on 9 July 1739, he sailed for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a> with six ships and on 21 November 1739 took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portobelo">Portobelo</a> from its 90-man garrison.</p><p>The Patriots opposed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-walpole">Robert Walpole</a> hailed it as vengeance for Admiral Hosier's failed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> of 1726 to 1728, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-arne">Thomas Arne</a> composed &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rule-britannia">Rule, Britannia!</a>&quot; during the celebrations of 1740, and a tower commemorating the victory was put up by members of the Vernon family at Hilton Hall outside Wolverhampton.</p><p>The assault on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cartagena-colombia">Cartagena de Indias</a> failed after the attack on the fortress of San Felipe de Barajas was beaten back on 20 April 1741, with disease, heavy casualties and the rainy season forcing a withdrawal. British losses came to some four thousand five hundred dead and six ships. A descent on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> followed: Vernon took Guantánamo Bay, briefly renamed Cumberland Bay, but the march on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> de Cuba was abandoned, and his quarrels with Major General Thomas Wentworth ended with both recalled at the end of 1742.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Elected for Ipswich in his absence, Vernon commanded the North Sea Fleet in 1745 against the threat of a French landing in support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-edward-stuart">Charles Edward Stuart</a>. Two pamphlets attacking the Admiralty led to his removal from the flag list on 11 April 1746, but he kept his seat until his death at Nacton on 30 October 1757. His fighting instructions influenced later commanders. His 1740 order to dilute the sailors' rum gave grog its name, and Lawrence Washington, who had served under him as a marine captain, named his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> estate Mount Vernon in his honour.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Edward Vernon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Edward Vernon</b> captured Portobelo in 1739.</blockquote><a href="/state/real-audiencia-of-panama">Real Audiencia of Panama</a></li><li><blockquote>Cartagena's fortifications withstood the British attack under <b>Edward Vernon</b> in 1741, led by Blas de Lezo and Viceroy Sebastián de Eslava.</blockquote><a href="/state/viceroyalty-of-new-granada">Viceroyalty of New Granada</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Westminster, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Nacton, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.5,-0.13&amp;year=1731" aria-label="Westminster on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Edward Vernon"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4993 5724h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4993 5724h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.5,-0.13&amp;year=1731">What was here in 1731? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
